{"type":"text","timestamp":1794322346850,"sessionID":"ses_e3a0f5faeffeaja4roFLLuM44w","part":{"id":"prt_1c5f2334b0015QIYnp6rOB1w7J","messageID":"msg_1c5f2271c001DNby6ZfbC7UG24","sessionID":"ses_e3a0f5faeffeaja4roFLLuM44w","type":"text","text":"<think>Hmm, the venv creation was denied, likely because it writes into /workspace? No... The rule denies external directories except /tmp/opencode/*. The venv is in /tmp/opencode/work/venv which should be allowed. Maybe the denial was about the pip install writing to cache dirs, or about `python3 -m venv` creating files elsewhere (e.g., ~/.local). Let me try with a different approach: install into a venv inside the workspace? The workspace is fine to write to (it's the project). But I shouldn't commit a venv. I could create it at /workspace/.venv and add to .dockerignore (already has **/.venv ignored). That's a clean approach.\n\nLet me try creating the venv in /workspace/.venv.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794322346827,"end":1794322346846}}}
